        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> Thanks for creating this!\r\n> \r\n> I am not a user of 'leap.nvim' and those highlights were created based on [their descriptions](https://github.com/ggandor/leap.nvim/blob/0c2f6a27d0706fa86f2512bb0c470b68876e55fe/doc/leap.txt#L287). To my eye screenshots look usable, but if you feel strongly about it not being visible enough, I'll look into possibly updating them.\r\n> \r\n\r\nI don't have a strong opinion here, though as hours pass by, it's nice to have one less mental overhead to tire you out. ;)\r\n\r\n> In the meantime, you can add underline in every color scheme by creating the following autocommand **before** applying first color scheme:\r\n> \r\n> ```lua\r\n> vim.cmd('au ColorScheme * hi LeapMatch gui=underline')\r\n> ```\r\n\r\nThanks! I already did so prior to opening this issue. In fact, I pondered whether opening an issue was worth it. However, in the end, I concluded that color should not take precedence over plugin-intended attributes, so I decided to proceed-\r\n\r\nFor reference: https://github.com/ggandor/leap.nvim/blob/0c2f6a27d0706fa86f2512bb0c470b68876e55fe/fnl/leap/highlight.fnl#L82",
